accusing someone of narcissism has  become one of the great and tempting  insults of our age it's what we're  inclined to label anyone who appears  overweening boastful entitled or  excessively pleased with their own  existence but it's possible to wonder  whether the word really fits the  conceited and bombastic types we've  grown used to applying it to and a  larger point whether we're really  understanding and therefore correctly  addressing the origins of the  regrettable behavior to which it refers  behind our enthusiastic use of the term  lies an implicit theory of human nature  that people undue attention for  themselves grow pretentious and inclined  to bombast when at heart they feel an  extraordinary love for who they are it's  their hugely high estimation of  themselves combined with a reversed  pride and everything they are and do  it's at the root of their tendencies to  talk up their own achievements and to  try to secure the admiration of  strangers but on closer examination it  seems that the agitation and peacock  displays cannot really stand from a  feeling of being perfectly and required  ly enamored of Y itself those whom we  loosely call narcissists can't actually  think so well of themselves they boast  and show off because they can't find a  way of being sufficiently content with  who they really are and are driven on by  a manic haunting terror of their own  invisibility and mediocrity the  behaviors we associate with narcissism  the vanity the greed the lies they  aren't symptoms of self-love they are  proof of its dangerous  tragic absence let's recall the original  Greek myth from where the word descends  narcissus was a hunter from central  Greece the son of the river God's  Ephesus and the nymph liriope II he had  long flowing locks deep blue eyes  unblemished skin and full enchanting  lips one day as he  walks past a quiet pool of water he  caught sight of his own reflection  failed to recognize himself and was in  trance with the image he had never seen  anyone quite so enchanting and couldn't  stop falling profoundly in love with  himself the most admirable and rich in  being he'd ever spotted framed like this  narcissism sounds laughable and absurd  but derogatory remarks make us lose  sight of the genuine benefits of having  been able when young under the tutelage  of kindly caregivers properly to admire  oneself without reserve to have been  allowed to strut and sing and feel  utterly at home in one's own skin and  for a time delight amply in the sheer  wondrousness for one's own existence  there might be such a thing as good  narcissism a phase of coming to love  oneself that's the precondition for  knowing how to love anyone else most of  the problems of the world are created by  people who do not love themselves who  cannot inwardly believe in their own  Worth those who grew up with a searing  hatred and shame about who they were who  cannot now stop insistently claiming an  attention of which they were once  deprived one has to hate oneself really  rather a lot before it ever seems like a  good idea to devote one's life to trying  to prove valuable in the eyes of  complete strangers by contrast the  really self-contented ones among us are  those who have no burning need for  adulation and know how to draw quiet  satisfaction from nothing more than  their own unadorned ordinary being we  should want to build a world not where  there are fewer people who have loved  themselves but where there are many more  a world richer in what we can term  healthy narcissism we will be modest and  self-effacing ready to be quietly  interested in and attentive to others  happy to forego baubles and manic  acclaim once we've had a chance to gaze  at our own reflection for a while and  think it really pretty nice nice enough  for us then to go out and start  generously to  kindness and selflessness to those  around us did you know that the school  of life is actually a place ten places  in fact campuses all over the world from  Melbourne to London Taipei to Istanbul  with classes and books and lots more  please click on the link below to  explore more
 

